    "doi": "https://doi.org/10.1038/s41558-018-0231-9",
    "abstract": null,
    "field": "Climate change ecology",
    "problem": "The potential for fine-scale microclimates to buffer species against warming is poorly understood because coarse-scale extinction risk models fail to incorporate local topographic heterogeneity, hindering the development of robust in situ conservation strategies.",
    "method": "The authors model population persistence versus extirpation for 430 species using five million distribution records as a function of warming rate and topographic microclimatic heterogeneity, while controlling for recorder effort, other environmental drivers, and spatial autocorrelation.",
    "task": "The task is to empirically test whether microclimatic heterogeneity has buffered species against regional extirpations linked to recent climate change, using over five million distribution records for 430 species across England.",
